Dynamic Social Absorptive Capacity: A Multiscale Theory of Institutional Resilience Under Population Influx

Version 0.4 — Rigorous Derivations, Full Proofs, and Control Integration

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek 

Abstract

We present a complete mathematical theory of institutional stability in the face of large‑scale human mobility. The key construct is the Dynamic Social Absorptive Capacity (DSAC), a latent macroscopic order parameter that captures the instantaneous capacity of a society to integrate newcomers without systemic breakdown. The society is modeled as an adaptive multilayer network with vector‑valued node states. A Fractal Institutional Operator (FIO) formalises the hierarchical aggregation of local signals and the downward propagation of policy. We derive the DSAC dynamics rigorously: starting from the microscopic stochastic agent system, we take the mean‑field limit, perform a moment closure with a fast‑network adiabatic reduction, and obtain a low‑dimensional nonlinear ordinary differential equation. We prove that, under mild conditions, the DSAC compression operator is uniquely determined (up to an affine transformation) by a set of physically motivated axioms. A full Lyapunov stability theorem is established for the coupled network‑FIO system, yielding an explicit critical threshold on institutional responsiveness. We develop a nonlinear state estimator (unscented Kalman filter) to track the latent DSAC from imperfect measurements and formulate a model predictive control scheme for optimal policy under constraints. Parameter identification and a digital‑twin simulation pipeline are outlined. The theory is intentionally general: while migration is the primary case study, the same mathematical structure applies to any external perturbation—pandemics, technological disruption, climate shifts—making DSAC a universal theory of institutional resilience.

