1. Introduction

Human societies are perpetually perturbed by flows of people, ideas, and technologies. The question “how much change can a society absorb before its institutions fracture?” is one of the oldest in political science. Yet current answers remain fragmented, qualitative, and almost entirely retrospective. We argue that this is fundamentally a problem of dynamical systems and control. A society’s capacity to absorb newcomers is not a static attribute; it is a time‑varying state that can be modelled, measured, and steered.

We introduce Dynamic Social Absorptive Capacity (DSAC) as a rigorous order parameter. Unlike earlier metaphorical treatments, DSAC is mathematically defined as the output of a compression operator acting on the full social system state. We embed it in a multiscale coupled dynamical system that co‑evolves individual trust, social ties, institutional policies, and migration flows. The result is a falsifiable, predictive theory that can guide real‑time governance.

Version 0.3 established the main scaffolding. The present version 0.4 supplies all missing mathematical proofs, tightens the definitions, extends the model to vector‑valued states, and refines the control and estimation components to meet the standards of an applied mathematics journal. We provide:

· A rigorous mean‑field derivation of the DSAC equation from agent‑based dynamics.
