2. Mathematical Preliminaries

Let G(t) = (V, E(t)) be a time‑dependent weighted directed graph. The node set V has fixed cardinality N (native population) plus a time‑varying set of migrant nodes added at discrete times. Each node i carries a state vector \mathbf{x}_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d whose components represent institutional trust, economic integration, civic participation, health status, etc. We assume all components are normalised to [0,1]. The weight w_{ij}(t) \in [0,1] quantifies the directed social tie from i to j. Self‑loops w_{ii} capture self‑reinforcement.

Graph operators. The adjacency matrix \mathbf{W}(t) has entries w_{ij}. The out‑degree matrix \mathbf{D}^{\text{out}} is diagonal with D^{\text{out}}_{ii} = \sum_j w_{ij}. The Laplacian is \mathbf{L} = \mathbf{D}^{\text{out}} - \mathbf{W}. For a vector \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{N}, the consensus term \mathbf{L}\mathbf{x} measures local differences.

Norms. \|\cdot\| denotes the Euclidean norm for vectors and the spectral norm for matrices. \|\cdot\|_F is the Frobenius norm.

Empirical measure. For a population of N nodes, the empirical measure is \mu^{(N)}_t = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \delta_{\mathbf{x}_i(t)}.

Graph limits. We consider sequences of dense graphs that converge in the cut norm to a graphon \mathcal{W}:[0,1]^2\to[0,1]. The associated graphon Laplacian will appear in the mean‑field limit.

