3. Microscopic Adaptive Network Model

3.1 Node Dynamics

Each node i obeys a stochastic differential equation:

d\mathbf{x}_i = \Big[ \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_i) + \kappa \sum_{j} w_{ij}(t) \, \mathbf{V}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{x}_j) + \sum_{l=1}^{L} \mathbf{B}^{(l)} \mathbf{u}^{(l)}_i(t) \Big] dt + \Sigma \, d\mathbf{W}_i(t). \tag{1}

· \mathbf{f} : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^d governs intrinsic dynamics; typically \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}) = -\boldsymbol{\mu} \odot \mathbf{x} with elementwise decay rates.
· \mathbf{V}(\mathbf{x}_i, \mathbf{x}_j) = \boldsymbol{\Phi}(\mathbf{x}_j - \mathbf{x}_i) is a bounded influence function. We often take \boldsymbol{\Phi}(\mathbf{z}) = \mathbf{z} (linear diffusive coupling) but saturating forms may be used for robustness.
· \mathbf{B}^{(l)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d} maps the policy signal \mathbf{u}^{(l)}_i(t) from institutional layer l to node i. This signal is generated by the FIO (Section 4).
· \Sigma is a constant noise matrix; \mathbf{W}_i are independent standard Wiener processes.

3.2 Tie Strength Dynamics

Social ties adapt to state similarity and policy interventions:

\frac{d w_{ij}}{dt} = \eta \, w_{ij} (1 - w_{ij}) \Big( - \| \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{x}_j \|^2 + \theta + \pi_{ij}(t) \Big). \tag{2}

Here \eta>0 is the adaptation rate, \theta \in \mathbb{R} is the homophily threshold (ties strengthen when similarity exceeds this level), and \pi_{ij}(t) is an institutional boost to cross‑group ties (e.g., mixed housing subsidies). The logistic factor w_{ij}(1-w_{ij}) keeps the weight in [0,1].

3.3 Migration Events

At a sequence of times \{t_m\}, a batch of M_m new nodes is added. Each new node i is initialised with \mathbf{x}_i(t_m^+) \sim \rho_0(\mathbf{x}), a distribution possibly concentrated at low trust values. Initial outgoing ties are assigned randomly among existing nodes, with a bias toward nodes of similar origin. Incoming ties from the native population evolve according to (2) thereafter.

