4. Fractal Institutional Operator (FIO)

4.1 Hierarchical Partitions and Restriction/Prolongation

Let the node set V be equipped with a nested sequence of partitions \mathcal{P}^{(0)}, \mathcal{P}^{(1)}, \dots, \mathcal{P}^{(L)}, where \mathcal{P}^{(0)} consists of singletons and \mathcal{P}^{(L)} is the trivial partition \{V\}. Level l has n_l blocks. The restriction operator R_l : \mathbb{R}^{n_l} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_{l+1}} maps a signal on the blocks of level l to level l+1 by averaging:

(R_l \mathbf{y})_B = \frac{1}{|B|} \sum_{b \subset B} |b| \, y_b,

where B is a block at level l+1 and the sum runs over its sub‑blocks b. The prolongation operator P_l : \mathbb{R}^{n_{l+1}} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_l} is piecewise constant injection:

(P_l \mathbf{z})_b = z_{B(b)}.

Define the full up‑ and down‑ward operators:

\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow} = R_{L-1} \circ \cdots \circ R_0 : \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^{n_L}, \qquad
\mathcal{F}_{\uparrow} = P_0 \circ \cdots \circ P_{L-1} : \mathbb{R}^{n_L} \to \mathbb{R}^N.

These satisfy \mathcal{F}_{\downarrow} \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow} = I_{n_L} (exact reconstruction of constants) and the fractal consistency R_{k} \circ \cdots \circ R_l = R_{k,l} for any k>l.

4.2 Spectral Properties

Lemma 1 (Contractivity). For any vector \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^N orthogonal to the all‑ones vector \mathbf{1},

\|\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow} \mathbf{x}\| \le \rho \|\mathbf{x}\|,

where \rho = \max_{l} \|R_l\| < 1 unless the hierarchy is completely balanced and all blocks are equal sized; in that case \rho = 1 but the projection onto the orthogonal complement of \mathbf{1} has norm strictly less than 1.

Proof. Each R_l is a linear projection when normalised by block sizes. Its singular values are all \le 1 with at least one strictly less than 1 if the partition is non‑trivial and the vector is not constant across blocks. The composition inherits this property. ∎

The FIO condition number \kappa(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) on the subspace orthogonal to constants influences how uniformly local shocks are damped.

4.3 Institutional Feedback

At each level l, a controller observes the aggregated error \mathbf{e}^{(l)} = R_{l:0}(\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}^*) and generates a policy signal \mathbf{u}^{(l)} = -K_l \mathbf{e}^{(l)}. These signals are then prolongated down to the fine level and summed:

\mathbf{u}_{\text{pol},i} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \big( \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow}^{(l)} \mathbf{u}^{(l)} \big)_i,

where \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow}^{(l)} prolongates from level l to level 0. The overall effect is a linear feedback \mathbf{u}_{\text{pol}} = -\mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} (\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}^*), with \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} = \sum_{l} \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow}^{(l)} K_l R_{l:0}. This operator is symmetric positive semidefinite with respect to a weighted inner product.

