6. Lyapunov Stability of the Full Multiscale System

We now prove that the coupled microscopic network and FIO feedback loop is stable when the institutional gain exceeds a critical threshold.

6.1 Linearised Dynamics

Linearise (1) and (2) around the desired equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* = \mathbf{1} (all states maximal), w_{ij} = w_{ij}^* (strong in‑group ties, weak cross‑group). The deviation \mathbf{z}_i = \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{1} satisfies

\dot{\mathbf{z}}_i = -\boldsymbol{\mu} \mathbf{z}_i - \kappa \sum_j L_{ij} \mathbf{z}_j - (\mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z})_i + \text{higher order}.

The Laplacian \mathbf{L} is evaluated at the equilibrium weights. Because \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} is positive semidefinite, the closed‑loop system matrix \mathbf{M} = \mathbf{I}_d \otimes (\boldsymbol{\mu} + \kappa \mathbf{L} + \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}}) is positive definite.

6.2 Multiscale Lyapunov Function

Define the Lyapunov functional

V(\mathbf{z}) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_i \mathbf{z}_i^\top \mathbf{z}_i + \sum_{l=0}^{L-1} \frac{\nu_l}{2} \| R_{l:0} \mathbf{z} \|^2,

where \nu_l \ge 0 are chosen later, and R_{l:0} maps the fine grid deviations to level l. The first term penalises individual deviations; the remaining terms penalise spatial inequalities at each hierarchical level, reflecting institutional costs.

6.3 Time Derivative and Stability Condition

Taking the derivative along trajectories and ignoring noise and nonlinear terms, we obtain

\dot V = -\mathbf{z}^\top \Big( \mathbf{M} + \sum_l \nu_l R_{l:0}^\top R_{l:0} \mathbf{M} \Big) \mathbf{z}.

Because R_{l:0} is a contraction (Lemma 1), the matrix in parentheses is positive definite provided the gains in \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} are large enough. More precisely, let \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M}_0) be the smallest eigenvalue of the non‑FIO part (\boldsymbol{\mu} + \kappa \mathbf{L}). We require

K_{\min} > \frac{1}{\sigma_{\min}^2(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow})} \Big( \beta m_{\max} - \lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{M}_0) \Big),

where \sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) is the smallest non‑zero singular value of the restriction to the orthogonal complement of constants, and m_{\max} is the maximum migration rate.

Theorem 2 (Exponential Stability). Under the above condition, there exist constants c_1, c_2 > 0 such that

V(t) \le V(0) e^{-c_1 t} + \frac{c_2}{\text{gain}}.

In particular, the DSAC S(t) converges exponentially to a neighbourhood of the equilibrium S^*, whose size is inversely proportional to the institutional gain.

Proof. Full details in Appendix C. We use the weighted inner product to bound the migration disturbance as an additive term, and apply Gronwall’s inequality. ∎

Corollary 1 (DSAC Collapse Criterion). If K_{\min} falls below the critical threshold, the system undergoes a saddle‑node bifurcation; no stable positive equilibrium exists, and S(t) drifts towards an absorptive capacity collapse.

