7. Observability and Nonlinear State Estimation

The DSAC S(t) is not directly measurable. We observe a vector \mathbf{y}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^p of socio‑economic indicators (e.g., aggregated trust surveys, unemployment claims). The measurement model is

\mathbf{y}(t) = \mathbf{H} S(t) + \boldsymbol{\nu}(t),

with \boldsymbol{\nu} white Gaussian noise. Since S is scalar, observability holds if any component of \mathbf{H} is non‑zero. For a multidimensional DSAC, the pair (F,H) must be observable.

Because the dynamics (4) are nonlinear, we advocate an unscented Kalman filter (UKF) for state estimation. The UKF avoids linearisation errors and captures the non‑Gaussian effects induced by the cubic term. The filter propagates a set of sigma points through the dynamics and measurement equations, updating the mean and covariance.

The continuous‑discrete UKF algorithm is summarised in Appendix D. Under persistence of excitation (sufficient variation in m(t)), the filter error is exponentially bounded.

