10. Broader Implications and Ethical Framing

The mathematical framework developed here is not limited to migration. Replace “migration rate m(t)” with “rate of technological unemployment,” “pandemic shock,” “climate‑induced displacement,” or “demographic aging,” and the equations remain structurally identical. DSAC thus becomes a universal theory of institutional resilience under exogenous perturbations. Migration is its first, most pressing application.

We reiterate that the theory describes institutional processes and network effects, never the innate characteristics of individuals or groups. The model’s parameters capture the responsiveness of governance, the plasticity of social ties, and the design of integration policies—factors that are under societal control. The stability condition derived in Theorem 2 shows that collapse is not inevitable; it is a consequence of insufficient institutional adaptation. The mathematics thus carries a hopeful message: with the right investments in responsive, multilevel governance, societies can remain stable even under substantial demographic change.

