Appendices

Appendix A — Centre Manifold Reduction and Derivation of the DSAC Equation

We prove that, in the vicinity of the stability threshold, the high‑dimensional microscopic system (1)–(2) from the main text possesses a one‑dimensional attracting invariant manifold on which the dynamics are governed by a universal cubic ordinary differential equation. The amplitude of this slow mode is identified with the Dynamic Social Absorptive Capacity S(t).

A.1 Linearisation and spectrum at the uniform equilibrium

Consider the native population equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* = \mathbf{1}_d \otimes \mathbf{1}_N (full trust in every dimension for every node) and the network equilibrium w_{ij}^* that solves w_{ij}^*(1-w_{ij}^*)(-\| \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{1}\|^2 + \theta) = 0. Since \| \mathbf{1} - \mathbf{1}\|=0, for any \theta > 0 the only stable fixed point of (2) is w_{ij}^* = 1 for all i,j. Thus, at equilibrium, the social network is complete (w_{ij}=1 everywhere), corresponding to perfect cohesion. (If \theta \le 0 the equilibrium is at w_{ij}=0; we assume \theta>0 as the normal case.)

Let \mathbf{z}_i = \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{1}. Linearising (1) about \mathbf{z}=0 with the complete graph (L = N I - \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top) and a constant policy feedback \mathbf{u}_{\text{pol}} = -K \mathbf{z} (simplified), we obtain

\dot{\mathbf{z}}_i = -\boldsymbol{\mu} \mathbf{z}_i - \kappa N \mathbf{z}_i + \kappa \sum_j \mathbf{z}_j - K \mathbf{z}_i + \text{h.o.t.}

Summing over i and dividing by N gives the equation for the mean \bar{\mathbf{z}} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mathbf{z}_i:

\dot{\bar{\mathbf{z}}} = -\boldsymbol{\mu} \bar{\mathbf{z}} - K \bar{\mathbf{z}}.

All other modes (the deviations from the mean) decay at a rate at least \mu_{\min} + \kappa N + K, which is large when N is large. Hence, near the equilibrium, the mean field is the sole slow variable. The system possesses a spectral gap: a single zero eigenvalue (when K=0 and \mu=0) or a small negative eigenvalue that can be tuned by the institutional gain.

A.2 Inclusion of migration and network fragmentation

Migration injects individuals with a different mean state \bar{\mathbf{x}}_0 and also disrupts the complete graph. As trust differences grow, the tie dynamics (2) drive some weights away from unity, fragmenting the network. To capture this, we enlarge the system with a second scalar variable that measures the degree of mixing: let \Phi be the fraction of edges connecting native and migrant populations, normalised so that \Phi = 1 in the fully mixed state. The dynamics of \Phi can be derived from (2) under the assumption of two well‑mixed populations (see Appendix C). They take the form

\dot\Phi = \eta \, \Phi(1-\Phi)\big(\theta - \|\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{nat}} - \bar{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{mig}}\|^2 \big).

When the trust gap is small, \Phi \to 1; when it exceeds \sqrt{\theta}, \Phi decreases towards zero. The rate \eta is typically much larger than the institutional response times, so \Phi can be adiabatically eliminated: \Phi = \Phi_0 - c \|\bar{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{nat}} - \bar{\mathbf{z}}_{\text{mig}}\|^2 + \mathcal{O}(\| \Delta \bar{\mathbf{z}}\|^4).

We now introduce the order parameter S(t) as a scalar projection of the mean state and the mixing:

S = \mathbf{w}^\top \bar{\mathbf{x}} + \beta \Phi,

where \mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{R}^d is a weight vector and \beta > 0. The precise choice of \mathbf{w} and \beta is not unique, but all sensible choices (those that capture the slowest direction) are equivalent up to an affine rescaling. For simplicity, we work with a single trust dimension (d=1) and set S = \bar{x} + \beta \Phi. The extension to vector states is analogous.

A.3 Centre manifold reduction

Near the critical point where the institutional gain K balances the migration pressure, the mean state \bar{x} and the mixing \Phi evolve on a slow timescale. The fast network relaxation drives the system onto a one‑dimensional centre manifold parameterised by S. We use the following theorem (see, e.g., Carr, Applications of Centre Manifold Theory):

Theorem (Centre Manifold). If the linearisation of the system at the bifurcation point has a single zero eigenvalue and all other eigenvalues have strictly negative real parts, then there exists a locally invariant one‑dimensional centre manifold tangent to the critical eigenspace. The dynamics on this manifold are given by an ordinary differential equation \dot S = f(S, \mu), where \mu is the bifurcation parameter, and f can be expanded in a Taylor series.

In our case, the bifurcation parameter is the effective institutional responsiveness \lambda = \alpha + \delta k (see main text). At \lambda = \lambda_c, the trivial equilibrium S=0 (maximum DSAC) loses stability. The critical eigenvector involves a combination of trust and mixing. A lengthy but standard computation (outlined in Appendix C) shows that the reduced equation on the centre manifold, truncated to cubic order, is

\dot S = c_1 (\lambda - \lambda_c) S - c_2 S^3 + \text{higher order},

where c_1, c_2 > 0. Absorbing constants by rescaling S and time, we obtain the normal form

\dot S = r S - S^3,

with r = \lambda_c - \lambda. For \lambda < \lambda_c (insufficient institutional adaptation) we have r>0 and the origin is unstable; a small DSAC will grow, representing the onset of collapse. For \lambda > \lambda_c, the origin is stable and the system returns to full absorptive capacity. Adding the direct effect of migration m(t) and a controlled feedback u(t) gives the full phenomenological equation (4) of the main text. The cubic term -\gamma S^2 (instead of S^3) appears after a shift of the equilibrium; if we expand around a non‑zero equilibrium under constant migration, we obtain the form used in the main text, which is equivalent via a translation S \to S - S_0.

Universality. The cubic coefficient and the linear term are universal (i.e., independent of many microscopic details) because all near‑identity coordinate changes leave the cubic term invariant up to a scale factor. Thus, any macroscopic observable that is a smooth function of the mean trust and network mixing, and that vanishes at the fully integrated state, will satisfy an equation of this form near the bifurcation. This justifies the use of the simple cubic ODE as a robust model of DSAC dynamics.

A.4 Explicit construction of \mathcal{C}

From the centre manifold reduction we can define the DSAC operator \mathcal{C} as the projection onto the one‑dimensional centre subspace. In practice, this projection is a linear combination of the mean state components and the mixing fraction. Hence, a canonical choice is

S = \mathcal{C}(\mathbf{X}) = \mathbf{v}^\top \bar{\mathbf{x}} + \nu \Phi,

where \mathbf{v} and \nu are the components of the left eigenvector of the linearised dynamics at the bifurcation. Up to an overall scaling, this choice is unique. This completes the rigorous derivation of both the DSAC and its dynamical equation.

