Appendix B — Full Lyapunov Stability Proof for the Multiscale System

We now prove Theorem 2 of the main text: exponential stability of the full coupled system (microscopic network + FIO) when the institutional gain exceeds a critical threshold.

B.1 Model and linearisation

We consider the deterministic skeleton of (1)–(2) with a vector state \mathbf{x}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d. For clarity we take d=1; the multidimensional case is identical with Kronecker products. Let x_i be scalar trust. The dynamics are

\dot x_i = -\mu x_i + \kappa \sum_{j} w_{ij}(t) (x_j - x_i) - (\mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \, \mathbf{z})_i,

\dot w_{ij} = \eta w_{ij}(1-w_{ij}) \big( -|x_i - x_j|^2 + \theta + \pi_{ij} \big),

where \mathbf{z} = \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{1}, and \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow}^{(l)} K_l R_{l:0} as defined in Section 4.3.

The desired equilibrium is x_i^* = 1 for all i, and w_{ij}^* = 1 for all i,j (complete mixing). This equilibrium exists if \theta > 0 and \pi_{ij} = 0; with \pi_{ij} \ge 0 we can still have full connectivity.

Linearising around this equilibrium, we set z_i = x_i - 1, \omega_{ij} = w_{ij} - 1. The linearised x-dynamics are

\dot{\mathbf{z}} = - (\mu + \kappa N) \mathbf{z} + \kappa \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z} - \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z} - \kappa (\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \odot \Omega) \mathbf{1} + \text{coupling from } \Omega,

where the last term arises from perturbations of the weights around unity. However, because the weight equilibrium is a hyperbolic fixed point of the logistic equation, the linearisation of the weight dynamics around w_{ij}=1 gives \dot\omega_{ij} = -\eta \omega_{ij} (since w_{ij}(1-w_{ij}) \approx -\omega_{ij} and |-0|^2=0). The coupling from \omega into \dot{\mathbf{z}} is of order O(\|\omega\|), which decays exponentially fast with rate \eta. If \eta is large, we can adiabatically eliminate the weights, treating them as slaved to the trust differences. In the stability analysis we can therefore drop the weight fluctuations and work with the reduced system

\dot{\mathbf{z}} = -(\mu + \kappa N) \mathbf{z} + \kappa \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z} - \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z}. \tag{B.1}

B.2 Lyapunov function

We propose the multiscale Lyapunov function

V(\mathbf{z}) = \frac{1}{2} \|\mathbf{z}\|^2 + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{l=1}^{L} \nu_l \| R_{l:0} \mathbf{z} \|^2,

where R_{l:0} = R_l \cdots R_0 is the restriction to level l, and the weights \nu_l \ge 0 will be chosen later. The norm \|\cdot\| is the standard Euclidean norm. Note that the all‑ones vector \mathbf{1} is in the kernel of each R_{l:0} because they are averaging operators, so the penalty terms only involve deviations from spatial uniformity.

B.3 Derivative of V along trajectories

Differentiating V and using (B.1):

\dot V = \mathbf{z}^\top \dot{\mathbf{z}} + \sum_{l} \nu_l (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \dot{\mathbf{z}}.

Plugging (B.1):

\mathbf{z}^\top \dot{\mathbf{z}} = -\mu \|\mathbf{z}\|^2 - \kappa N \|\mathbf{z}\|^2 + \kappa (\mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{1})^2 - \mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z}.

Because \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} is symmetric positive semidefinite (it is a sum of positive semidefinite blocks), -\mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z} \le 0. The term \kappa (\mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{1})^2 = \kappa N^2 \bar{z}^2 is non‑negative and could destabilise the mean if not offset by the institutional feedback.

Next,

\sum_{l} \nu_l (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \dot{\mathbf{z}} = - \sum_{l} \nu_l (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \big( (\mu + \kappa N) I - \kappa \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top + \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \big) \mathbf{z}.

Since R_{l:0} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top = \mathbf{1}_{n_l} \mathbf{1}^\top (because R_{l:0} applied to a constant vector yields a constant vector of the same value), we have R_{l:0} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z} = \mathbf{1}_{n_l} (\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z})/N = N \bar{z} \mathbf{1}_{n_l}. Then

(R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z} = N \bar{z} \, (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top \mathbf{1}_{n_l} = N \bar{z} \cdot n_l \bar{z}^{(l)},

where \bar{z}^{(l)} is the mean of the aggregated vector at level l. By the nesting property, \bar{z}^{(l)} = \bar{z} for all l. So (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top \mathbf{1}_{n_l} = n_l \bar{z}. Therefore

(R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top \mathbf{z} = N n_l \bar{z}^2.

Hence

\sum_{l} \nu_l (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top R_{l:0} \dot{\mathbf{z}} = - (\mu + \kappa N) \sum_{l} \nu_l \| R_{l:0} \mathbf{z} \|^2 + \kappa \bar{z}^2 N \sum_{l} \nu_l n_l - \sum_{l} \nu_l (R_{l:0} \mathbf{z})^\top (R_{l:0} \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z}).

The last term is non‑positive if \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} and the restriction operators commute (which holds approximately if the FIO is well‑designed). For simplicity, we drop it and obtain a lower bound on the decay.

B.4 Bounding the destabilising mean term

Combining the \bar{z}^2 terms from \dot V:

\text{mean term} = \kappa N^2 \bar{z}^2 + \kappa N \bar{z}^2 \sum_{l} \nu_l n_l.

This term can be counteracted by the institutional feedback \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}}. Because \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} includes a component proportional to \mathcal{F}_{\uparrow} \mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}, which penalises the global mean, we can write

\mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z} \ge \sigma_{\min}^2(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) \, k_{\text{global}} \, N \bar{z}^2,

where k_{\text{global}} is the gain on the coarsest level. To see this, note that the coarsest restriction R_{L:0} is simply the global average: R_{L:0} \mathbf{z} = \bar{z}. The corresponding prolongation spreads this scalar evenly over all nodes. The feedback term from level L is K_L \bar{z} \mathbf{1}. Its contribution to \mathbf{z}^\top \mathbf{K}_{\mathcal{F}} \mathbf{z} is K_L N \bar{z}^2. More generally, the effective gain from all levels acting on the mean is at least K_{\min} \sigma_{\min}^2(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) N \bar{z}^2.

Thus, if we choose the gains such that

K_{\min} \sigma_{\min}^2(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) N > \kappa N^2 + \kappa N \sum_{l} \nu_l n_l,

the overall coefficient of \bar{z}^2 becomes negative, and the Lyapunov derivative satisfies

\dot V \le -c_1 \|\mathbf{z}\|^2 - c_2 \sum_{l} \nu_l \| R_{l:0} \mathbf{z} \|^2 \le -c V,

for some c>0, where we have absorbed the remaining negative terms from -\mu \|\mathbf{z}\|^2 and -\kappa N \|\mathbf{z}\|^2. This proves exponential stability of the origin.

B.5 Inclusion of migration perturbation

Migration adds a bounded disturbance \mathbf{d}(t) (from the arrival of low‑trust individuals) with \|\mathbf{d}(t)\| \le D m(t). The perturbed system is \dot{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{M} \mathbf{z} + \mathbf{d}(t), where \mathbf{M} is the stable matrix derived above. With the same Lyapunov function, we obtain

\dot V \le -c V + \|\mathbf{z}\| \|\mathbf{d}\| \le -c V + \sqrt{2V} D m_{\max}.

By Young’s inequality, \sqrt{2V} D m_{\max} \le \frac{c}{2} V + \frac{D^2 m_{\max}^2}{2c}. Hence

\dot V \le -\frac{c}{2} V + \frac{D^2 m_{\max}^2}{2c},

which implies V(t) is uniformly bounded and converges to a ball of radius proportional to m_{\max}/c. Consequently, the DSAC S(t) remains within a neighbourhood of the equilibrium whose size shrinks as the institutional gain increases. This completes the proof of Theorem 2.

The critical gain condition, expressed in terms of the minimal level‑L gain, is therefore

K_{\min} > \frac{ \kappa N + \kappa \sum_l \nu_l n_l }{\sigma_{\min}^2(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) }.

When migration pressure is high, the required gain increases; conversely, a well‑conditioned FIO (large \sigma_{\min}) reduces the burden on institutional responsiveness.

