Appendix D — Spectral Properties of the Fractal Institutional Operator

Lemma 1 (Contractivity). For any vector \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^N orthogonal to \mathbf{1},

\|\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow} \mathbf{x}\| \le \rho \|\mathbf{x}\|, \qquad \rho = \max_{l} \|R_l\| < 1,

where R_l are the normalised restriction operators (with appropriate block‑size weights). The smallest non‑zero singular value \sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{F}_{\downarrow}) on the orthogonal complement of constants satisfies \sigma_{\min} \ge \prod_{l=0}^{L-1} \sqrt{\frac{n_{l+1}}{n_l}}, with equality when blocks are balanced.

Proof. Define the weighted inner product \langle \mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y} \rangle_{D_l} = \mathbf{x}^\top D_l \mathbf{y} where D_l = \operatorname{diag}(|b|) for blocks at level l. Then the normalised restriction \tilde R_l = D_{l+1}^{-1/2} R_l D_l^{1/2} is an orthogonal projection (since it averages with weights). Consequently, its singular values are either 0 or 1. The composition \mathcal{F}_{\downarrow} has singular values given by the products of the singular values of each \tilde R_l, but because they act on different subspaces, the overall singular values are the product of a subset of them. In particular, any vector orthogonal to constants lies in the complement of the coarsest subspace, and at each step, its projection onto the constant subspace of that level is removed. The worst‑case contraction is given by the product of the largest singular value less than 1 at each level, which is \max \sqrt{n_{l+1}/n_l} (when blocks are balanced). For non‑balanced partitions, the contraction is stronger. The smallest non‑zero singular value corresponds to the mode that is averaged the least, giving the lower bound stated. ∎

The condition number \kappa_{\perp} = \sigma_{\max}/\sigma_{\min} on the orthogonal complement determines how uniformly information dissipates. A well‑balanced administrative hierarchy yields \kappa_{\perp} \approx 1, ensuring that no local shock is disproportionately amplified.

