Appendix F — Model Predictive Control Formulation

We provide the complete discrete‑time MPC formulation used in the digital twin.

F.1 Discrete‑time model

The DSAC ODE is discretised with a sampling period \Delta t:

S_{k+1} = f(S_k, m_k, u_k) = S_k + \Delta t \big( -\alpha S_k + \beta m_k - \gamma S_k^2 + \delta u_k \big).

The migration rate m_k is assumed known (from forecasts) over the horizon. The control u_k represents the aggregated institutional effort (e.g., integration spending), subject to constraints:

u_{\min} \le u_k \le u_{\max}, \quad \text{and possibly rate constraints } |u_{k+1} - u_k| \le \Delta u_{\max}.

F.2 Cost function

We minimise

J = \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \big( S_k^2 + \rho u_k^2 \big) + S_N^2,

where N is the prediction horizon. The terminal cost S_N^2 is a control Lyapunov function for the unconstrained system, ensuring stability (see Rawlings & Mayne, Model Predictive Control: Theory and Design).

F.3 Optimisation

At each time step, given the current state estimate \hat S_k, the MPC solves:

\min_{\{u_k\}} J \quad \text{s.t. dynamics, constraints.}

This is a small‑scale nonlinear program, easily solved with sequential quadratic programming (SQP) or interior‑point methods. The first control u_k^* is applied, and the process is repeated at the next sampling instant (receding horizon).

Stability guarantee. Since the system is input‑affine and the stage cost is positive definite, and the terminal cost is a local control Lyapunov function, the closed‑loop system is asymptotically stable provided the horizon is sufficiently long and no state constraints are active (Mayne et al., 2000). In the presence of migration disturbance, the system converges to a neighbourhood of the origin whose size is proportional to the disturbance magnitude.


